(project pricklypear-prim-seam
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: prim-seam — the Prim_call seam: EVAL IS LOCAL, PRIMS ARE REMOTE. (Renamed from borge/remote-eval.borg 2026-09-04: the old name inverted the truth it specified — see the rename agent note below.) The CAPABILITY/LIBRARY that lets a client process sync the one tree's AST + data down, eval Nopales locally, and proxy out-of-working-set prim calls up to the image. The seam is one Prim_call effect handler swapped for an HTTP proxy; the image keeps the corpus, the data plane authority, and the merge index. No surface, no chat — the AGENT product (agent.borg) links this; so would a debugger, test harness, or future CLI.")
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-04 RENAME + SURFACE RECONCILE: chapter renamed borge/remote-eval.borg -> borge/prim-seam.borg. Rationale: the old name INVERTED the truth it specified — eval happens LOCALLY in the client; what is remote are the PRIM CALLS (/api/prim). The inversion did real damage: (a) readers landing from remote/lib/proxy_rim.ml found the transport story but had to follow a forward ref into local-first.borg to discover the branching story (lens = fork, sync = graft) — the local-first mechanism was effectively misfiled; (b) 'remote-eval' read as a product ('eval remotely from elsewhere -> needs a UI'), which is precisely how pp-remote grew the duplicated chat surface the 2026-12 alignment pass had to order retired. All cross-refs chased (borge/*.borg, pricklypear.borg, docs/cs-curriculum.md); historical notes below keep their 2026 dates and now say 'prim-seam' — this note is the global rename record. Package remote/ + binary pp-remote KEEP their names — 'remote' still accurately names talking to the remote image; no dune churn. SAME PASS: agent-loop subsection flipped implemented -> deprecated per the 2026-12 alignment decision (pp-remote's chat surface + /code lens browser retire into agent.borg; deleting remote/lib/surface.ml + http_ui.ml and collapsing the duplicated chat is the pending make). Its --serve chat verify smoke is DROPPED — it passed only because the retired surface still runs, and an 'implemented' stanza asserting ownership of a screen contradicted the 'implemented' positioning stanza in the same chapter. NOT deprecated: the loop SEAM (llm/ shared package, Loop.host) — agent.borg links it; what dies here is only the claim that prim-seam owns any screen. Mechanism/policy split reaffirmed: prim-seam = the Prim_call seam MACHINERY (substrate, server-endpoint, client, caching, placement, stepping); local-first = the lens POLICY (fork/sync/conflicts), pointing here, never re-spec'd.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-12 ALIGNMENT PASS (product vs capability — the line the code was reaching for). HISTORY: prim-seam arrived 2026-08-14 explicitly as 'NOT a new product — a mode of the Prim_call seam the agent host already uses, with the rim replaced by an HTTP proxy. agent/ is the local-only instance; pp-remote is the relaxed instance.' The code forked instead: pp-remote grew a chat surface + /code browser + --serve (a second product), and the two hosts duplicated the chat templates, the serve functions, and the t-records. The duplication is a SYMPTOM of a missing noun, not an independent design. ALIGNMENT: (1) prim-seam is the CAPABILITY/LIBRARY: sync the AST/data down, eval locally, proxy out-of-working-set prims up to the image, three-way merge on reconnect. NO surface, NO chat. (2) The AGENT (agent.borg positioning) is the PRODUCT that links it: one process, one screen, own-forked branch, local-first edits synced up, installable browsers. (3) pp-remote shrinks back to this library + a minimal /eval harness (a capability DEMO, not a product); the chat surface + chat/* templates + /code lens browser the prior session built MOVE to the agent (one copy), and the shared llm/ loop the agent already links stays shared. (4) local-first.borg already owns the merge/conflict/identity/semantics-skew open work (sync-conflicts); prim-seam points at it, does not re-spec it. WHAT THIS PASS DOES NOT DO: restatus the implementation subsections (client, caching, placement, stepping, agent-loop) or touch code — this redraws the POSITIONING line only; a make pass retires pp-remote's product surface, collapses the duplicated chat into one on the agent, and re-roots the agent's eval path on local-first (own-fork, sync-push, offline-tolerant). The top-level doc's old phrase 'the client keeps ... the interactive surface (REPL, stepping debugger, agent loop)' was the conflation this pass fixes: the interactive surface + agent loop belong to the PRODUCT (agent.borg); prim-seam is the capability the product is built on.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-14 chapter created from the prim-seam design discussion. Thesis: the interpreter is a pure portable library (~few kloc OCaml, no service deps); the corpus (function_defs, type_defs, data_rows) is branchy rows in Postgres. They are separated along exactly the right line — the cheap-to-copy thing travels, the expensive-to-replicate thing stays home. Remote-eval is NOT a new product: it is a mode of the same Prim_call seam the agent host already uses, with the rim handler replaced by an HTTP proxy. agent/ is the local-only instance today; pp-remote --image=URL is the relaxed instance. Statuses are honest: the substrate is implemented (the seam, run_with_rim, read_eval_all_direct, the api-eval JSON boundary from i-fields-as-string 2026-08-12), everything else is planned.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-14 RECONCILE: a shared codec already exists — pure/prims_pure.ml (pricklypear-pure-prims, wrapped false as Prims_pure) defines value_to_json AND json_to_value (the inverse), explicitly shared between the image and a browser client per its dune comment. So the substrate codec is DONE; the remaining codec work is the ERROR codec (Interp_error/Prim_error/Reader_error -> JSON with kind/name/line/col, currently inline in image/lib/http_pages.ml api_eval) factored into pure/ + its inverse, plus a round-trip property test. ALSO RECONCILE: borge/structural-editing.borg client-eval already plans a browser client/ package (interp.js, js_of_ocaml --effects=cps, pure-prims-only, optimistic structural ops reconciled via op-protocol). client-eval and prim-seam share the seam (same pure interpreter, prims_pure factored out) but DIFFER: client-eval leaves DB prims UNBOUND in the browser and reconciles via op-protocol; prim-seam PROXIES DB prims over HTTP. Complementary, not duplicate. To avoid the client/ package-name collision (client/ is a tracked browser package), prim-seam's native host is the remote/ package (binary pp-remote), NOT client/.|) *)
