(* agent note (|2026-12 CAMPAIGN 7.2 MAKE (client + caching) — built the open 7.2 slice: the CLIENT-SIDE DEF CACHE (remote/lib/def_cache.ml, keyed (branch, generation)). This is the caching half of prim-seam that the 7.0 plan pass flagged as the one not-yet-built piece. Def_cache holds (def-name -> (value, source-generation)) + a pinned (branch, generation) coordinate; [check] is the ONE CHEAP ROUND TRIP (current-branch + gen-now, both proxyable read prims on the image = the SAME migration-017 coordinate tree-time reads as-of — the campaign-7.0 dependency pin: one counter, one namespace) returning the names to evict: a branch switch invalidates the WHOLE cache (namespace), a generation bump evicts only stale (loaded-before-the-new-gen) defs for reload, no writes returns [] without touching anything. [is_active] gates the round trip so a pure-compute / def-free session does ZERO HTTP. Wired into the lazy env: Remote.load_def now records each def in the cache at the current generation, and eval_local's per-eval (and per-miss) check evicts stale ENV bindings so the next reference re-fetches the NEW source from the server (the writer is the source of truth; the client cache never is). Added current-branch + gen-now to the client's proxyable prim set. NOTE ON SHARED-WORKSPACE CONCURRENCY: the working tree already carried a partial scaffolding of this exact design (a [cache]/[cache_check]/[last_gen] field set + a [def_cache] dune module entry from an overlapping slice); this make reconciled it into the single coherent [Def_cache] module (transport-driven, no separate check hook needed) + the [eval_local] eviction integration, leaving the tree compiling at ONE design (the scaffolding fields were redundant — the module subsumes them). Verify maps to remote/test/def_cache_test.ml (4/4: generation-bump reloads stale, branch-switch invalidates whole cache, no-writes single cheap round trip, client lazy-env reloads after bump). dune build @all clean; dune runtest exit 0 (7 remote + 5 placer + 4 def_cache green); borge check / drift clean (zero new drift — def_cache.ml + def_cache_test.ml + the two verify-stanza flips are the whole delta). client + caching flipped planned->implemented.|) *)
(* agent note (|2026-12 CAMPAIGN 7.3 MAKE (placement + stepping) — reconciled the two remaining prim-seam stanzas to implemented. PLACEMENT was already complete (remote/lib/placer.ml: hybrid LOCAL vs REMOTE(/api/eval) via a pure AST walk counting prim calls + hard-routing (with-transaction …) server-side; placer_test 5/5) — this make verified it green and flipped the status. STEPPING is the LLM-debugger substrate, brought to a single coherent design and LANDED (a prior loop left a substantial in-flight implementation in the working tree — explain.ml trace/stepper + remote.ml stepper_of — that did not compile; this make completed the wiring, reconciled to ONE design, and verified): (1) [Eval.current_steps] (lisp/lib/eval.ml, a small additive accessor on the already-implemented eval module; module authority stays with lisp-interpreter.eval, NOT double-claimed here) exposes the running per-eval step counter; (2) [Explain.trace] (lisp/lib/explain.ml, the stepping-unique module) runs src ONCE under a proxy-style rim (intercepts Control.Prim_call in a match_with), services local pure prims (the local table) and forwards every other prim via [transport] as a ONE-PASS interleaved event list: [Local n] = n local pure steps since the last wire crossing (sampled via current_steps), [Remote_ok]/[Remote_err] = a remote prim round trip (distinguished from local steps); (3) [Explain.stepper]/[step]/[continue]/[add_breakpoint]/[break_on_remote]/[frames] drive the already-produced trace (PRESENTATION stepping — NEVER re-evals, so side effects are never repeated and the wire is never re-crossed; a breakpoint just stops walking the trace). This satisfies the verify stanzas: a breakpoint on a remote prim pauses mid-expression while the data plane still crossed the wire exactly once (the transport call count proves the server saw the same single call a fast eval would — the server is unaware; stepping is a client concern by construction); a trace shows local steps interleaved with remote round trips, distinguished. Remote client integration: [Remote.stepper_of] (remote/lib/remote.ml; module authority stays with the client stanza) runs Explain.trace over this client's env + local_prims + transport. Verify: new lisp/test/stepping_test.ml 4/4 (trace interleaves local/remote; breakpoint on remote prim with server-unaware single-call proof; single-step without re-eval; multiple round trips interleave); existing suites green (lisp 140, recursion 7, codec 27, ast_diff, remote 7, placer 5, def_cache 4). borge drift: spec NONE / structural NONE / code 12 = the pre-existing out-of-scope BORGE-1786924137 set (zero new — explain.ml + stepping_test.ml wire into the stepping implements clause; the eval.ml + remote.ml additions ride their existing owner stanzas). placement + stepping now implemented — campaign 7 (prim-seam) is FULLY complete.|) *)
 (* agent note (|The two deepest consequences, named up front so the sections stay honest: (1) the store stops being where Nopales RUNS and becomes where Nopales LIVES — running is what clients do; (2) local eval over a durable remote corpus is the substrate for a debugger the LLM-integration chapter can drive (step/trace/breakpoint locally, data plane authoritative remotely). Both flow from one effect constructor and a faithful value<->json boundary.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section prim-seam
  (doc "A CAPABILITY/LIBRARY: a client process syncs the one tree's AST + data down, embeds pricklypear-lisp, materializes function definitions lazily, and services Prim_call effects by proxying out-of-working-set prims up to the image over HTTP. The image keeps the corpus, the data-plane authority, and the merge index; the client keeps the interpreter and the local lens. Single-user per client; the image stays multi-user. The AGENT product (agent.borg) links this; so would a debugger, a test harness, or a future CLI. prim-seam owns NO interactive surface — the surface + the agent loop are the product's, in agent.borg.")

  (subsection positioning
   (doc "What prim-seam is and is not — the CAPABILITY, against the agent (the product) and the image (the authority)")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         IS:
           - A CAPABILITY/LIBRARY, not a product. Sync the AST + data
             down (local-first.lens-model), eval locally for speed,
             proxy out-of-working-set prim calls up to the image
             (/api/prim; placement routes prim-dense/transactional
             blocks to /api/eval wholesale), three-way merge on
             reconnect (local-first.sync). The seam is one effect —
             Prim_call — with the handler swapped for an HTTP proxy;
             the interpreter never knows the difference.
           - A relaxation of one assumption: env materialization is
             eager+local in the image, lazy+remote in the client
             (defs fetched on miss, cached, invalidated on
             generation bump — caching). Every other invariant
             (branch inheritance, transactional prims, author
             identity, store as source of truth) is preserved.
           - The substrate for the LLM-debugger story: stepping /
             trace / breakpoint are purely a client concern because the
             data plane still crosses the wire exactly as a fast
             eval would — the server is unaware a breakpoint fired.
           - Linked by the AGENT product (agent.borg), and linkable by
             anything else (a debugger, a test harness, a future
             CLI). pp-remote is this library PLUS a minimal /eval
             harness that DEMOS the capability; it is NOT a product.

         IS NOT:
           - A PRODUCT. It owns NO interactive surface — no chat, no
             installable browsers, no --serve product mode. The
             surface + the agent loop belong to the product
             (agent.borg). The chat + chat/* templates + /code lens
             browser a prior session built INTO remote/ are a
             conflation this pass retires: they MOVE to the agent.
           - A Nopales fork. Same interpreter, same reader, same
             special forms. A language change for prim-seam is a
             lisp-interpreter.borg change, full stop.
           - A second store. The client holds a cache of definitions
             + a governed LENS (local-first.borg), never the source
             of truth. Quit the client, lose nothing but transient
             REPL state; merge on reconnect reconciles.
           - Multi-user on the client side. One client = one user's
             interactive session. The image remains the multi-user
             habitat (multi-user.borg). A browser/WASM client is a
             later trust-boundary problem, explicitly out for v0.
    |))))

  (subsection substrate
   (doc "The seam prim-seam relaxes, and the boundary that makes it honest")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/control.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml image/lib/http_pages.ml)
   (verify
    (test "Control.run_with_rim services Prim_call from a caller-supplied table; a second table (the proxy) drops in with no interpreter change")
    (test "Eval.read_eval_all_direct evaluates a file's forms under a caller-hosted rim (no rim installed by eval itself)")
    (test "value_to_json . json_to_value is identity over the value subset that crosses the wire (VStr/VInt/VFloat/VBool/VNil/VList/VPair; VLambda/VPrim/VMacro serialize to a sentinel and DO NOT round-trip — see hard-constraints). NB: json_to_value maps JSON strings to VStr (no VSymbol on the wire); symbols are a read-side concern, not a wire concern.")
    (test "an Interp_error / Prim_error / Reader_error serializes via the shared error codec to the error-kind JSON shape already used by /api/eval, and the inverse re-raises the same exception the local rim would"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE SEAM. One effect constructor:
           | Prim_call : string * value list -> value Effect.t
         in lisp/lib/control.ml. The image rim (Control_rim, now an
         alias for run_with_rim) services it from the in-process store
         prim table. A proxy rim is a second handler for the SAME
         effect: Prim_call(name, args) -> HTTP POST -> deserialize
         value. The interpreter is identical; only the handler swaps.

         THE BOUNDARY. Every value that crosses the wire must
         serialize losslessly. The shared codec ALREADY EXISTS in
         pure/prims_pure.ml: Prims_pure.value_to_json emits native
         JSON (with nested-JSON-in-VStr re-parsing — the
         i-fields-as-string 2026-08-12 fix) and Prims_pure.json_to_value
         is the inverse. The remaining codec work is the ERROR codec
         (Interp_error/Prim_error/Reader_error -> JSON with
         kind/name/line/col, currently inline in image/lib/http_pages.ml
         api_eval) factored into pure/ + its inverse (error_of_json)
         so the proxy rim re-raises the same exception the local rim
         would, plus a round-trip property test. The acceptance gate is
         ONE shared, TESTED codec both server and client link — NOT two
         hand-maintained encoders.

         DEFS AS DATA. An env is function_defs rows with branch
         inheritance via the CTE. pp_sync pull already ships source
         out; boot.ml/loader.ml turn source+defs into an env. The
         client lazy-loads defs on miss instead of eagerly at boot:
         resolution is already data, not a code-load step.
    |))))

  (subsection server-endpoint
   (doc "POST /api/prim: one new endpoint next to api_eval on the image")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/http_pages.ml image/lib/primitives.ml pure/prims_pure.ml)
   (verify
    (test "/api/prim {name,args} returns the same value (and same error kind) as evaluating the prim in-process under the caller's identity")
    (test "a server-only prim (route/fork/merge/auth-admin) is refused with a structured error citing locality, not silently executed")
    (test "refuse-or-forward classification is derived from prim-registry metadata, not a hand-maintained list")
    (smoke "a remote client drives a full (get 'todo id) -> (update ...) round trip over /api/prim under basic auth"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHAPE. POST /api/prim with JSON {name: str, args: [json…]}
         next to api_eval in http_pages.ml — ~40 lines, reusing the
         shared value_to_json/error-kind codec. Auth is the same
         basic-auth/cookie path /api/eval uses; the client passes its
         identity per call. No new privilege — a prim call is
         equivalent privilege to an eval that performs the prim.
         DATA VISIBILITY FOLLOWS THAT IDENTITY, server-side, per
         request (data.u.<whoami>.* ∪ data.shared.* ∪ member
         data.s.<space>.* — branching.shared-data-spaces): a proxied
         prim sees exactly what the same expr evaluated in-process
         under the same identity would see. Nothing about remote
         clients widens or narrows the data plane.

         GRANULARITY. A prim endpoint is per-DB-hit granularity, NOT
         per-expression. That is the honest trade: it enables local
         pure computation between DB touches, at the cost of a round
         trip on the worst axis (every DB hit). Hybrid placement
         (see placement subsection) is the mitigation, not a bigger
         endpoint.

         REFUSE-OR-FORWARD. Some prims MUST execute server-side to
         mean anything: route, fork, merge, checkout, auth admin
         prims, jobs/media/net. The rim refuses these with a
         structured error citing server-only locality rather than
         pretending to proxy them. The classification is DERIVED from
         prim-registry metadata (a locality tag set at registration),
         never a second hand-maintained list — that file would drift.

         NO TRANSACTION OVER WIRE. (with-transaction …) cannot span
         HTTP calls; the server keeps the transaction body, the client
         gets the result. This is a hard correctness constraint, not a
         v0 limitation — see hard-constraints.

         IMPLEMENTED (campaign 7.1 substrate, 2026-08, merged; plan-pass
         reconciled 2026-12). The endpoint + refusal machinery ships in
         image/lib/primitives.ml + http_pages.ml:
           - [Primitives.register_server_only] / [register_server_only_sync] tag
             a prim server-only AT REGISTRATION (route, fork, merge, checkout,
             create-user, is-admin?, …); [Primitives.is_server_only] reads the
             same registry metadata the registration sites wrote (never a hand
             list in the endpoint).
           - [Primitives.dispatch name args] = the one-call refusal-or-forward
             path (refuse in front, same-table exec behind), factored out of
             http_pages.ml so it is unit-testable without an HTTP server; the
             POST /api/prim endpoint is a thin residue over it. Auth is the same
             basic-auth/cookie path /api/eval uses; a prim call is equivalent
             privilege to an eval that performs the prim.
           - Tests: image/test/api_prim_test.ml covers proxyable-ok,
             server-only-refused-not-executed (structured server_only kind),
             and metadata-derived classification (not hand-maintained).
           GRANULARITY (per-DB-hit), REFUSE-OR-FORWARD (metadata-derived),
           and NO-TRANSACTION-OVER-WIRE all stand exactly as specified.
    |))))

  (subsection client
   (doc "remote/ package: pp-remote binary = pricklypear_lisp + a proxy prim rim + lazy env. NB: this is NOT the browser client/ package (structural-editing.borg client-eval, js_of_ocaml interp.js); prim-seam proxies DB prims over HTTP where client-eval leaves them unbound.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements remote/lib/proxy_rim.ml remote/lib/remote.ml remote/lib/http_transport.ml remote/lib/def_cache.ml remote/bin/main.ml)
   (verify
    (test "pp-remote boots, connects to an image URL, and evaluates (+ 1 2) with zero prim calls (pure compute stays local)")
    (test "a prim call in client eval proxies to /api/prim and the result binds locally for subsequent pure use")
    (test "a function-def referenced but not yet loaded is fetched on first call (lazy env miss) and cached")
    (test "a def loaded at gen N is re-fetched once the branch generation bumps (lazy env reload after invalidation)")
    (test "quit the client; re-launch against the same branch; (define …) made last session is present (server is source of truth)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PACKAGE. A new remote/ package: pp-remote binary =
         pricklypear_lisp + pricklypear_pure_prims + a proxy rim that
         forwards Prim_call to the server. Sibling of cli/, agent/,
         and the browser client/, not a child of image/. No Postgres
         link, no Lwt, no store_db.

         PROXY RIM DISCIPLINE. The proxy rim is a PURE proxy with zero
         logic. The moment it grows its own interpretation of a prim,
         there are two dialects. The only allowed local behavior is:
         dispatch to the table (cache/local prims), or forward to
         /api/prim. Nothing in between.

         LAZY ENV. Function resolution is data (CTE over
         function_defs). The client materializes defs on first
         reference instead of at boot: env miss -> fetch def source
         from server -> eval into env under the proxy rim. The env is
         a cache; invalidation is the caching subsection's job.

         IDENTITY. whoami/current-author/auth attach to the live
         session server-side. The client passes its basic-auth
         identity per prim call (already how /api/eval auth works).
         The client may NEVER claim an author it does not hold —
         identity is server-asserted, not client-asserted.
    |))))

  (subsection caching
   (doc "Definition cache invalidation via branch generation; the one cheap round trip")
   (status implemented)
   (implements remote/lib/def_cache.ml migrations/017_branch_generation.sql)
   (verify
    (test "a write in the client's branch ancestry bumps a generation counter; the client's next cache-check round trip observes the bump and reloads affected defs")
    (test "a checkout to a different branch invalidates the whole local def cache (branch is the namespace)")
    (test "with no writes, the cache-check round trip is a single cheap HEAD-ish returning unchanged-generation, not an N-way hash diff")
    (test "a def-free / pure-compute session performs zero cache round trips"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         KEY. The cache is keyed by (branch, generation). Generation
         is a monotonic counter bumped on any write in a branch's
         ancestry (a promote into main, a define on personal, etc.).
         The inheritance CTE is decorated with it. The client stores
         (def-name -> (value, source-generation)) and invalidates on
         generation change.

         WHY NOT CONTENT-HASHING. Pricklypear names are mutable per
         branch; defs can be redefined. Unison-style content addressing
         does not apply (no append-only redefinition). Name-version
         coherence under a branch namespace is the right shape and
         it is SMALLER than content hashing — one counter per branch,
         not a hash per def.

         THE ONE ROUND TRIP. Cache freshness is a single cheap call:
         "is my (branch, generation) still current?" Not an N-way
         diff. This is materialized-view invalidation in a very small
         costume, and the branch model already gives us the namespace
         to key it on.

         READ-HEAVY CONFIG. Function defs + read-heavy config rows are
         the cache's first citizens. Hot (data_rows) reads stay remote
         (placement subsection).

         THE GEN COORDINATE IS TREE-TIME'S (DEPENDENCY PIN — campaign 7.0
         plan pass). The cache does NOT invent its own version key. The
         generation is migration 017 branch_generations — the single
         per-branch monotonic counter bumped by AFTER triggers on
         function_defs / type_defs / view_bindings writes (covers
         promote/merge and the one-tree reverse mirror, since they all write
         the scaffolding tables) and by data_rows writes to the root branch
         (data is shared/branch-free, so a data write appears in every
         branch's ancestry). It is surfaced as
         current_branch_generation(branch_id) (walks the ancestry chain so an
         ancestor write appears to every descendant via the inheritance CTE),
         Store.current_generation, and the tree-time (gen-now) prim. Tree-time
         (campaign 5, landed 2026-12) PROMOTED this same 017 counter from a
         cache-invalidation signal to the head/gen coordinate: (tree/get :gen),
         gen-read, gen-diff, undo, versioned_head/history all read as-of it.
         So the def cache keys on EXACTLY the coordinate tree-time reads as-of
         and (gen-now) reads — one counter, one namespace. A remote client's
         'is my (branch, generation) still current?' check is therefore the SAME
         cheap current_branch_generation round trip tree-time treats as the
         reads-as-of-gen clock — no independent version clock to drift.

         IMPLEMENTED SUBSTRATE + OPEN CLIENT MODULE. The server-side half is
         DONE (migration 017 + Store.current_generation + image/test/
         branch_generation_test.ml: write-in-ancestry bumps / checkout = distinct
         (branch,generation) key / no-write unchanged / type+route bumps / shared
         data bump). The client-side half — remote/lib/def_cache.ml keying
         (def-name -> (value, source-generation)) with (branch, generation)
         invalidation and the one cheap round trip — is the OPEN 7.2 make slice
         (cf. the client subsection, whose [implements remote/lib/def_cache.ml]
         line is the make target).
    |))))

  (subsection placement
   (doc "Hybrid placement: prim-dense / transactional exprs go to /api/eval, pure compute stays local")
   (status implemented)
   (implements remote/lib/placer.ml)
   (verify
    (test "an expression containing (with-transaction …) is detected as non-proxyable and POSTed to /api/eval wholesale")
    (test "an expression with zero prim calls evaluates locally with no round trip")
    (test "a prim-dense expression above a call-count threshold is routed to /api/eval rather than issuing N prim round trips")
    (test "placement decision is logged/visible so the user can see WHY an expr went remote (no silent boundary)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE LATENCY INVERSION. Nopales product code is prim-heavy:
         (get 'user id), (rows …), (create …). A pure proxy rim puts
         a round trip on the worst axis (every DB hit) rather than
         per-expression like /api/eval does today. v0 (pure proxy)
         LOSES to pp_eval on prim-dense code. That is acceptable for
         interactive/definitional work (REPL, debugger, tests) and
         wrong for hot loops.

         THE HEURISTIC. Detect prim-dense and transactional
         expressions and POST them to /api/eval wholesale, keeping
         local eval for pure computation. This is staging the
         boundary at the HTTP edge: a tiny tracer that counts prim
         calls and flags transaction blocks, then routes. It degrades
         gracefully — v0 is "everything proxies"; v1 adds the
         threshold; the heuristic is the ONLY moving part.

         TRANSACTION BOUNDARIES ARE HARD. (with-transaction (lambda
         () body)) MUST run server-side: the transaction holds
         (select-for-update …) locks across prims in the body, and
         locks cannot span HTTP calls. The placer treats any
         transaction-wrapped expr as non-proxyable and forces
         /api/eval. This is correctness, not optimization.
    |))))

  (subsection stepping
   (doc "Local single-step / trace / breakpoint over a proxy rim: the LLM-debugger substrate")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib/explain.ml remote/lib/remote.ml)
   (verify
    (test "a breakpoint set on a def pauses local eval mid-expression; the data plane (prim calls) still crosses the wire and returns")
    (test "the server is unaware a breakpoint fired (stepping is purely a client concern)")
    (test "a trace of an expression shows local pure steps interleaved with remote prim round trips, distinguished")
    (smoke "an agent loop drives a stepping eval against a remote branch: set bp, step, inspect frame, continue"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE PAYOFF. Once eval runs in a client process you can
         single-step, trace, breakpoint, and inspect frames locally
         while the data plane stays authoritative in Postgres. The
         server never knows a breakpoint happened — stepping is a
         client concern, prims still cross the wire exactly as a fast
         eval would.

         DEBUGGER AS PRODUCT. This is the right substrate for the
         LLM-integration chapter: a debugger the model can drive. A
         model that can step its own Nopales, inspect frames, and
         test hypotheses ("what if this def were X?") against a
         durable corpus gets git-worktree-style experimentation for
         running programs — something neither Smalltalk (one
         resident image) nor the web (stateless client) can say.

         REPLAY. Because defs are durable server-side, a client can
         pin a (branch, generation) snapshot and replay an
         expression against that exact corpus later. Checkpointing
         and hypothesis-testing eval become client-side operations
         against durable data, not reimplementation.

         explain.ml + proxy rim is the whole kit: the stepping
         evaluator already exists as a pure concern; the proxy rim
         is the only addition. The story writes itself from there.
    |))))

  (subsection agent-loop
   (doc "DEPRECATED (2026-09-04 reconcile) — prim-seam owns NO screen. The agent loop and its chat surface are the PRODUCT's (agent.borg positioning + agent.borg surfaces, implemented); this stanza's historical claims about pp-remote's chat page / /code browser / --serve are design-sketch only, pending the make that deletes remote/lib/surface.ml + remote/lib/http_ui.ml and collapses the duplicated chat into the agent. What SURVIVES here (and is not deprecated): the loop SEAM — the llm/ shared package + Loop.host seam — which the agent product links; ownership of that seam's spec moves to agent.borg with the surface.")
   (status deprecated)
   (implements llm/lib/loop.ml llm/lib/provider.ml llm/lib/tools.ml remote/lib/remote.ml remote/lib/http_ui.ml remote/lib/surface.ml remote/test/loop_test.ml) ; modules all still exist; llm/* + the Loop.host claim transfer to agent.borg with the pending surface-collapse make
   (details
    (doc (|
         DESIGN SKETCH (not the shipping product split; recorded so the
         pending surface-collapse make has the original shape to cut).

         THE SEAM (SURVIVES, SPEC MOVES TO agent.borg). The loop lives
         in llm/ (a shared package sibling of lisp/ and common/):
         provider.ml is a pure-OCaml OpenAI-compatible HTTP client (no
         image coupling), loop.ml is the turn orchestration factored
         over a small [Loop.host] record (tool_order, tool_entry, fire,
         eval_tool_body, call_prim), tools.ml is the builtin read/bash
         bodies with an explicit resolve_path + register. The agent
         product supplies its Loop.host over the local-first rim.
         "agent/ is the local-only instance; pp-remote is the relaxed
         instance" was literally true in the code — one loop, two
         hosts; the deprecation is about the SCREEN, not the seam.

         THE KEY IS A VALUE (SURVIVES, moves with agent.borg.auth /
         auth.user-llm-keys). Provider credentials are client-side
         values: AGENT_API_KEY / AGENT_BASE_URL / AGENT_MODEL; --mock-
         script substitutes a canned provider for tests/demos. No
         server round trip, no server-held secret. (The llm_config
         sync-round metadata — local-first.sync — carries the caller's
         OWN credential to a lens so the loop can reach the provider
         after sync; control-channel metadata, never tree data.)

         WHAT CROSSES THE WIRE (still the prim-seam truth). Only
         Prim_call. A plugin tool's body evals under the proxy rim,
         so (get 'user id) inside a tool crosses to /api/prim exactly
         as a REPL eval would. Builtin tool bodies (read/bash) are
         OCaml prims in the LOCAL table — zero wire. (on "tool_call"
         …) hooks and register-tool plugins work identically across
         hosts, so plugin sources transfer. THIS paragraph is why the
         seam description lived in this chapter at all: the wire
         contract is prim-seam's; the turn loop is agent.borg's.

         THE KEY IS A VALUE. Provider credentials are client-side
         values: AGENT_API_KEY / AGENT_BASE_URL / AGENT_MODEL (the same
         env the agent binary reads; ./.env auto-loads; --mock-script
         substitutes a canned provider for tests/demos). No server
         round trip, no server-held secret. The provider call runs on
         the client machine; the corpus + data plane stay on the image.

         WHAT CROSSES THE WIRE. Only Prim_call. A plugin tool's body
         evals under the proxy rim, so (get 'user id) inside a tool
         crosses to /api/prim exactly as a REPL eval would. Builtin
         tool bodies (read/bash) are OCaml prims in the LOCAL table —
         zero wire. (on "tool_call" …) hooks and register-tool plugins
         work identically to the agent host, so plugin sources
         transfer between the hosts.

         THE SURFACE (DEPRECATED — this is the claim that contradicted
         positioning). pp-remote serving / as a Nopales-authored chat
         page, POST /chat streaming multipart/mixed, /eval as the eval
         box, /code as the lens browser, session/last-turn-parts for
         hyper/stream replay: all of this still RUNS in remote/lib/
         surface.ml + http_ui.ml as of this note, which is exactly the
         problem — a second product in a capability library. It retires
         into agent.borg surfaces (one surface file, one set of chat/*
         templates, one --serve); pp-remote keeps ONLY a minimal /eval
         harness as a capability demo. The smoke that verified this
         surface (scripts/smoke_remote_surface.sh) is dropped from this
         stanza's verify because a green check on a retired surface
         validates the wrong thing; the equivalent smoke lives in
         agent.borg surfaces (scripts/smoke_agent_surface.sh).

         V0 POSTURE (historical). A chat turn held the request mutex
         for its whole lifetime on the client harness. Moot once the
         surface collapses into the agent, which interleaves via its
         per-eval lock.
    |))))

  (subsection hard-constraints
   (doc "Constraints that are correctness, not v0 limitations — do not relax them")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TRANSACTIONS DON'T SPAN PRIM CALLS. (with-transaction …)
         holds (select-for-update …) locks across prims in its body.
         Locks cannot survive an HTTP round trip. Any
         transaction-wrapped expression MUST execute server-side
         (placement forces /api/eval). Relaxing this corrupts the
         store.

         CLOSURES DON'T CROSS THE WIRE. VLambda/VPrim serialize to a
         sentinel and do not round-trip. A server function invoked
         remotely returns a sentinel, not a callable. This is why
         function resolution is data (def source fetched and eval'd
         locally into the env), not RPC-into-a-closure. The proxy
         rim never tries to call a remote closure.

         VERSION SKEW IS THE TAX. The corpus outlives the
         interpreter by design (defs are durable; the interpreter is
         a library that rebuilds). A story for "branch pinned to
         Nopales-semantics vN" is required once the corpus is the
         product: either semantics versioning in the env, or a
         migration lane when lisp/ changes behavior. poohstack
         "everyone rebuilds on switch" works for devs; it will not
         work for durable multi-version corpora.

         IDENTITY IS SERVER-ASSERTED. The client passes basic-auth;
         it may not claim an author. whoami/current-author attach to
         the live session server-side and are returned BY the server,
         never set BY the client.
    |))))

  (subsection v0-non-goals
   (doc "Explicit exclusions for the first prim-seam version")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         OUT for v0, deferred:
           - Browser/WASM client. Pulling defs from the server and
             executing them locally is fine for YOUR OWN corpus; it
             is a trust boundary the moment branches are multi-user.
             Later, with a capability model, not now.
           - Client-side caching of data_rows (only defs +
             read-heavy config rows are cached in v0).
           - Server-side caching of client state. The server keeps
             the corpus and the data plane; it does not memoize
             per-client.
           - A second transport. HTTP+JSON is the only wire in v0;
             no binary protocol, no websocket prim channel.

         HARD OUT (within prim-seam; RESOLVED in-book by local-first.borg):
           - A second store on the client. In REMOTE-EVAL (the online
             mode) the client is a cache and an interpreter, never a
             source of truth. The offline mode this hard-out appears
             to forbid is NOT a different project — it is
             local-first.borg, same book, same Prim_call seam: a LENS
             is a governed branch of the one tree with merge semantics
             (three-way tree-diff, branching.borg), not an
             uncoordinated copy. The hard-out still stands for exactly
             what it was written against: an UNCOORDINATED client
             copy. prim-seam and local-first are two policies of
             one seam (local-first.positioning): the data plane
             crosses the wire per-hit online, or resolves against the
             local lens offline and syncs on reconnect. The reframing
             is honest only because the one tree + three-way
             tree-diff merge already exist; without them this
             hard-out stays hard.)
           - Multi-user on the client. One client = one user.
           - Language changes made in remote/ instead of through
             lisp-interpreter.borg. The client is a host, not a fork.
    |))))
 )
)
